const title = "How to Verify a Download with a Hash (SHA-256 Explained) - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "What a hash is, how to check a downloaded file against the publisher's SHA-256 checksum, and why SHA-1 is no longer trusted for security.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-to-verify-a-file-with-a-hash.html">
<aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
    <div class="wrap article">
      <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Security basics</p>
      <h1>How to Verify a Download with a Hash</h1>
      <p class="lead">A file fingerprint is a short code worked out from a file. If the file changes at all, the code changes too. This guide shows how to use that code to check a download is genuine.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>What the code is</h2>
<p>A file fingerprint is a short string of letters and numbers worked out from the whole file. Two identical files always give the same code. If even one part of a file changes, the code is completely different.</p>
<h2>How to check a download</h2>
<ol>
<li>Find the code the website publishes next to the download. It is often labelled SHA-256.</li>
<li>Use the tool to get the code for the file you downloaded.</li>
<li>Compare the two codes, letter by letter. If they match exactly, the file is genuine.</li>
</ol>
<h2>If the codes do not match</h2>
<p>Do not open the file. Download it again from the official website. If it still does not match, contact the website.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>Run the text 'abc' through the SHA-256 tool and you get ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad. Change one letter to 'abd' and you get a completely different code. That is how a changed file is spotted.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>What should I do if the codes do not match?</summary><p>Do not open the file. Download it again from the official website and compare again.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Which code should I compare?</summary><p>Use the one the website publishes, usually labelled SHA-256.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does a matching code mean the file is safe?</summary><p>It means the file is exactly the one the website published. Only download from places you trust.</p></details>
      </section>
